    <!-- Practical Yard Living & Free State Testing -->
    <section class="narrative-section">
      <h2>Free State Soil Testing &amp; Cleanup Programs</h2>
      <p>
        Under Washington's Model Toxics Control Act (MTCA), the Department of Ecology operates state-funded programs to protect homeowners:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Free Yard Sampling:</strong> Homeowners located within designated service areas can sign up for free professional soil sampling directly through the Department of Ecology or Tacoma-Pierce County Health Department.</li>
        <li><strong>Free Soil Replacement:</strong> If soil testing reveals elevated arsenic (above 100 ppm) or lead (above 500 ppm), the state provides free soil replacement for qualifying residential yards, removing contaminated topsoil and backfilling with clean soil and fresh sod.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Healthy Actions for Daily Yard Living</h2>
      <p>
        If you live in a smelter plume boundary, following these simple daily habits prevents soil ingestion and keeps your family safe:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Use Raised Beds for Vegetable Gardening:</strong> Build raised garden beds lined with weed fabric and fill them with fresh topsoil and compost for growing fruits and vegetables.</li>
        <li><strong>Maintain Ground Cover:</strong> Keep bare dirt covered with healthy lawn grass, wood chips, bark mulch, or gravel to prevent dust generation.</li>
        <li><strong>Wipe Boots &amp; Pet Paws:</strong> Place sturdy doormats at entryways, remove shoes at the door, and wipe pet paws to avoid tracking outdoor dirt indoors.</li>
        <li><strong>Wash Fresh Produce:</strong> Thoroughly wash homegrown fruits and vegetables before eating.</li>
      </ol>
    </section>

